  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Trending more humid with heavy downpours and \nlocalized flash flooding possible late this week into the \nweekend\n\nWeak sfc low (1018 MB) near KDCA, with an ill-defined warm \nfront extending NE along the I-95 corridor and an inverted \ntrough into the Lower and Middle Susq Valleys will be the focus\nfor several waves of heavy showers and a few TSRA through early\ntonight, while another sfc low(s) (1017 MB) over Central and \nSouthern Ohio will bring similarly slow-moving and training \nshowers/and a few TSRA, mainly west of the RT 219 corridor. \n\n12 hour POPS across our Laurel Highland zones will be 60-70% and \nin 70-80+% range across the Lower Susq Valley.\n\nMost of the CWA is in WPC's MRGL risk of Excessive Rainfall \n(Flash Flooding) for the next 5 days (with the entire state in a\nMRGL risk for Fri and Sat). \n\nWe expect PWATS to climb to +1-2 Sigma with corresponding sfc \ndewpoints rising into the 70s over the S and U60s in the N and \nstay there right through at least Tuesday. \n\nPWATs seem to peak this afternoon around 2.25\" in the SE. Then \nthey don't get that high again (daily peaks of 2\" in the SE, but\n1.7\" in the NW). A pattern shift to a more- zonal flow aloft \nshould occur over the weekend, but a cold front and/or dry air \nis not in store for us.\n\nThe confidence in flooding is low due to confidence in \nplacement of the heaviest rain being low. Certainly, the wettest\nplaces from over the weekend will sit higher on the list, and \nthere are some hints that the SErn third or so of the area will \nbe most at- risk for repeated shots of heavy rain. But, no flood\nwatch are expected for today, and we'll only get a better feel \nfor each new day as we see where the previous day's precip has \nbeen heaviest.\n\n   ------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Severe thunderstorms are possible Thursday and \nFriday afternoons/evenings\n\nMRGL risk for severe wind gusts exist on Thurs (and likely\nFriday, too) over the SE half of the area per the latest SPC \noutlooks (new DY3 not out at press time). The shear is less than\nmodest, but CAPE does climb into the 1000-2000 J/kg each of\nthose aftns. However, the CAPE is long/skinny, which is more\nindicative of heavy rain threat. If we can get some dry air in\nthe mid levels either day, and the small height falls on Friday\ncan help jack up the instability and DCAPEs, any of our storms\ncould make a strong to severe gust. \n \n   ------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Heat index could approach 100F Thursday and \nFriday SE of Harrisburg\n\nThe heat index does get very close to and may exceed 100 \ndegrees across a small portion of our SE zones using the \ncurrent NBM guidance temps/dews. However, clouds and the anticipated\nshowers/storms will keep the HI lower - even if just a degree \nor two which would make the difference in advy/no. The temps \nwon't be all that abnormal, it will be the dewpoints which would\ntake us over. At this point, we'll hold off on heat advisories.",
